you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's program  the billion pound gigafactory leading  the way in the uk's electric  car revolution he's back  arnold schwarzenegger hosts the austrian  world summit pledging to terminate  air pollution and how insects could be  the solution to reducing deforestation  in the amazon  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those developing the solutions  and we've got lots coming up today  including how  sea otters are helping to keep  california's marine ecosystem  healthy but first a one billion pound  electric vehicle hub  including a new battery gigafactory has  been announced by nissan  based in sunderland the car maker says  it will bring together electric vehicles  renewable energy and battery production  in a world  first ev manufacturing ecosystem  now the site which would be the uk's  largest gigafactory is expected to open  in 2024  and will produce 9 gigawatt hours of  battery capacity a year  that's enough to power 100 000 vehicles  now to put that into context the  existing sunderland plant  only produces 1.9 gigawatt hours of  batteries  at the moment but with the sale of new  petrol and diesel cars  banned from 2030 far more investment in  batteries and electric vehicles will be  needed  the society of motor manufacturers and  traders says at least  60 gigawatt hours of giga factory  capacity will be needed in the uk  by the end of the decade well our  business correspondent paul kelso has  more from nissan's sunderland plant  the shift to electrification here at  nissan has been driven by  two factors the government has said by  2030  new internal combustion engine vehicle  production is going to be  banned so they will have to be electric  and there's a brexit  factor too the deal done between the eu  and the uk government  means that before 2030 the bulk  of the electric vehicles value its  battery is going to have to be produced  in the uk or in europe or they will  attract tariffs  when they're exported so there are sound  financial reasons for this ship  the project to build a huge gigafactory  here is an attempt not just to green the  cars  but to green the entire process it's  intended to be 100  renewable energy fueled by huge  solar plant that's being built here  funded with 80 million  pounds of taxpayer money from sunderland  city council the government's also  putting in  tens of millions more so the shift to  electrification  while necessary won't be happening  without taxpayers  helping pay for it arnold schwarzenegger  has told sky news  countries need to show leadership on  climate change and stop waiting for  others to take action  he's hosting the austrian world summit  in vienna which brings together heads of  state and government  as well as business leaders and  activists to discuss efforts to reduce  global emissions well adam parsons is  there and adam and he's got a tough  message for world leaders then  yeah and they've been listening to him  this is a spanish riding school  in vienna one of the city's best known  buildings converted  for the summit today and the cast list  who's been here has included  presidents and prime ministers and  politicians alex sharma  was here on behalf of the government  nicholas sturgeon  among those coming but really the  standout performers that this audience  have been listening to will have been  two  of those uh one of them was arnold  schwarzenegger come to him in a moment  the other one was greta tumber  who launched the latest of her absolute  double barrel shotgun caustic criticisms  of governments politicians and what she  described as a  high income nation she said i've been  using  empty words rhetoric hadn't done  anything the list  went on i have to say alex sharma  nicholas sturgeon had to listen to that  and put on rather awkward smiles the  other person you'll be  expecting will be arnold schwarzenegger  and we caught up with him  to talk about climate change and of  course the forthcoming  cop 26 in the uk here's what he had to  say you shouldn't just  always think that the cop events  are the only solution it's a very good  solution but not the only one  we should also build a from the ground  up movement we should educate the public  so that they are with the programs that  they know what the dangers are  and the dangers that lie ahead and it  has nothing to do with politics it has  to do with one thing  let's go and get rid of pollution  because pollution  kills people the chinese and the indians  are  putting out enormous amount of issues  emissions they're not  here is there any point in the sense of  of really a climate change policies that  don't include  those two giant industrial nations well  i think that uh  we shouldn't limit ourselves and to  think that the only way we get things  done is  if everyone is in on it i don't remember  john f kennedy saying we are not going  to send the man to the moon  unless the chinese also send a man to  the moon unless the russians send the  main to the moon unless the european  center i mean  no he said we're going to be the first  ones and we're going to go and do it  so that's leadership so i think at this  meeting what you've really had is the  carrot  of mr schwarzenegger saying we've got to  be ambitious and the stick of greta  timber  saying young people around the world  expect action and they  are not seeing it one final thought  though from schwarzenegger he said lots  of people just don't understand all the  terminology they don't understand what  net zero is they don't understand what  carbon credits are  what you need is a single clear message  for him that message was this  get rid of pollution adam thank you  in today's other climate news the world  meteorological organization has  recognised a new  record high temperature for the  antarctic the 18.3 degrees celsius  reading was taken on the 6th of february  last year  at a research base on the continent's  northwest tip  one of the fastest warming regions on  earth the committee rejected an even  higher temperature of 20.75 degrees  which was reported three days later on  seymour island  rishi sunak has promised to tackle  climate targets by  forcing businesses to report their  environmental impact  speaking at manchin house the chancellor  said the new  integrated sustainability disclosures  requirements  will come into effect before the cop 26  conference in november  he also announced details of a new green  bond which the treasury hopes will raise  at least  15 billion pounds the queen has said  we're going to have to change the way we  do things during a visit to the  university of edinburgh's climate change  institute  the monarch arrived in style in an  electric hybrid land rover  and spoke to students and staff about  their research into ways to reverse the  effects of global warming  at the end of the visit her majesty was  presented with two trees that will be  planted as part of a special initiative  to celebrate her platinum jubilee next  year  and scientists say sea otters are  playing a big part in the fight against  climate change  the furry creatures are helping keep  california's marine forests healthy  by eating sea urchins whose numbers have  risen dramatically in the area  urchins had been over grazing on kelp  forests which play  a vital role in absorbing carbon dioxide  from the atmosphere  bangladesh is one of the world's most  densely populated countries with people  living along a delta of rivers that  empty into the bay of bengal  two-thirds of the country is less than  five metres above sea level  and climate change is threatening the  lives and livelihoods of those on the  country's coast  sea surface temperatures in the tropical  indian ocean rose by  one degree between 1951 and 2015.  the rate of warming is the fastest  amongst tropical oceans  and accounts for a quarter of the  increase in global oceanic heat  over the last two decades as the oceans  get warmer  sea levels rise due to a process called  thermal expansion that's when water  expands  as it gets hotter the rise in sea  temperatures and global warming are also  linked to the intensity of tropical  cyclones  between 2000 and 2018 there was a  significant rise in the frequency  of very severe cyclonic storms in the  area  and higher sea levels and receding  coastlines mean storm surges cause  even more damage skies caterina vitozi  has been visiting the villages  already suffering these effects of our  changing climate  including calabogie where houses have  been raised off the ground on bamboo  sticks  and the land divided by the rising river  on bangladesh's south coast homes don't  just flood  they disappear  we're heading to a village called  calabogie  surrounded by a rising river  battered by cyclones this land of mud  and sediment is being washed away  taking homes and hope along  [Music]  can  rising sea levels in the bay of bengal  downstream  mean river tides here are getting higher  storm surges are more intense and  dangerous  over there about 500 meters away is a  small island with homes on it  until about a year ago that was  connected  to this bit of land and it wasn't just a  road to walk on it was a stretch of land  that had homes on it where people lived  and when you see this and you think  about climate change goals and climate  change targets you like to think that  action can make a difference but really  for people living here it's already too  late  this is why bangladesh says the world  must act  with this year's climate conference  needed not to talk about what will  happen but about what's happening right  here  right now catherine of autopsy sky news  in kalibogi bangladesh  and a reminder that you can hear more  from katarina in bangladesh in this  week's episode of climate cast  it's out tomorrow and you can find it  wherever you normally get your podcast  from  now there might be a bit of a buzz  around this next story as researchers  have found that  insects could help stop deforestation in  the amazon  a study commissioned by wwf and tesco  found that using insect meal to feed  fish and livestock  could cut the uk's use of soy by a fifth  and help protect important habitats  in the process here's how  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  that's everything from us for today  coming up tomorrow the difficult choice  facing uganda as it struggles to  reconcile a huge oil project and the  economic benefits it would bring  with world-leading laws to protect  nature thanks for watching  see you then

